WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:16.688
Think of a straight road on a map. You only need two places, then a ruler. An equation like y equals two x plus five links x and y. Every pair that fits is a point, and the points make a straight line.

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:32.747
The easiest points are where the line crosses the axes. On the y axis, x is zero. On the x axis, y is zero. So put x equals zero to find the y crossing, and y equals zero to find the x crossing.

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:46.102
Y equals a number is a flat, horizontal line. X equals a number is an upright, vertical line. A line through the origin needs another point, because both crossings are the origin.

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:54.214
The exam says: draw the line with equation. Find two points for each line and rule it across the whole grid.

00:00:55.320 --> 00:01:07.840
Step one: put x equals zero and solve for y, then put y equals zero and solve for x. Step two: if both are the origin, or one is off the grid, choose another x.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:20.396
Step three: plot the points using the scale on the axes. Step four: join them with a ruler and extend across the whole grid. Label each line with its equation.

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:27.693
Step five: check one extra point. The trap is a sign slip, so take care with minus signs.

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:42.322
June twenty twenty three. Draw the lines y equals two x plus five, four y equals x minus eight, and five y plus three x equals thirty. Three marks.

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:53.627
Line one. x zero gives y five, so the point nought, five. y zero gives two x equals minus five, so x is minus two point five.

00:01:54.200 --> 00:02:03.218
Line two. x zero gives four y equals minus eight, so y is minus two. y zero gives x equals eight.

00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:13.662
Line three. x zero gives five y equals thirty, so y is six. y zero gives three x equals thirty, so x is ten.

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:19.814
Now plot the six points and rule each line across the whole grid. Label them.

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:36.042
Check. On line one, x equals minus four gives y equals minus three. On line two, four times minus three is minus twelve, which is minus four minus eight. Both lines pass through minus four, minus three.

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:44.810
A tempting slip is a sign error. In line two, zero equals x minus eight gives x equals eight, not minus eight.

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:53.673
So all three lines are drawn, ruled across the grid and labelled. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:54.800 --> 00:03:05.274
A common mistake is to draw only between the two plotted points. Later parts need the lines to meet, so rule right across the grid.

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:18.831
Now you try, from November twenty twenty. Sketch each line, and show where each crosses the axes. Write your answer, then choose Continue to Answer.

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:34.995
Line one is flat. It crosses only the y axis, at nought, six. Line two: x zero gives y ten. y zero gives x ten. So nought ten, and ten nought.

00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:43.057
Line three: x zero gives y minus five. y zero gives two x equals five, so x is two point five.

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:53.578
Sketch the lines through those points. A common wrong answer gives y equals six an x crossing. It never crosses the x axis.

00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:57.458
Remember: two points, ruler across the grid, label, check.
